## INDUSTRY 3: MANUFACTURERS & CPG (10-200 EMPLOYEES, $5M-$50M REVENUE)

### Evidence base
- CPG manufacturers: 15.2% of mean manufacturing revenue lost today due to delays, downtime, rework, quality deviations, suboptimal asset use (Schneider Electric 2026 survey of 1,453 global executives)
- Expected to worsen: 21.37% next year, 29.14% by 2030
- Today only 13% of CPG manufacturers say AI is embedded end-to-end in core operations
- By 2030, 37% expect AI to be core — tripling in 4 years
- 32.7% anticipate 50-74% ROI on AI projects; 7.9% forecast >100% ROI (payback under 1 year)
- 73% of CPG companies still rely on conventional time-series forecasting (65-75% accuracy)
- AI-powered demand sensing achieves 88-94% accuracy
- Manufacturing downtime costs CPG companies $2.1 billion annually
- Average OEE: 68% vs world-class 85%+ — gap represents enormous value creation
- 44% of CPG leaders looking to AI/ML; 43% to increasing automation (Rockwell 2025)
- McKinsey: strong majority of CPG companies piloted AI in at least one function, but only ~1 in 5 scaled beyond single use case
- Bain: 90% of CPG executives thinking about AI, only 6% have a plan for using it to create business value

### Specific bottleneck evidence
- **Demand forecasting** — 73% still use conventional methods at 65-75% accuracy. AI achieves 88-94%. PepsiCo: forecast accuracy jumped from 71% to 91% MAPE, finished goods inventory dropped 12% ($340M working capital released), stockouts fell 34%, safety stock costs declined 18%, $245M annual EBITDA impact from inventory optimization alone
- **Manufacturing downtime** — $2.1B annual cost to CPG. PE-backed beverage manufacturer: unplanned downtime fell from 340 hours to 89 hours (74% reduction), OEE jumped from 73% to 82%, maintenance costs declined 12%, $46.9M annual EBITDA impact
- **Quality control** — mostly manual inspection. AI visual inspection can automate. Defect rates, downtime, QC costs
- **Sales hierarchy updates** — Auxis case: 85% error rate in sales hierarchy update process, extensive rework. RPA automation eliminated the error rate, saved 2,000+ hours of tedious manual work per year
- **DOT compliance** — Auxis case: distribution supervisors manually pulling and uploading vehicle inspection reports and hours of service for 1,300+ drivers. RPA automation saved 9,300+ hours of work and $370,000+ per year. Identified 15% of reported violations as false
- **Force-ship order errors** — Auxis case: 90,000 errors out of 1.17M force-shipped orders in a single quarter (7.7% error rate). Teams spent 30 minutes reworking each order. Force-ship automation expected to deliver $5-7M in revenue recognition
- **Inventory management** — excess safety stock, preventable stockouts, unnecessary markdowns, promotional spend missing the mark
- **Data fragmentation** — "product information lives in regional spreadsheets and disconnected PIM systems; orders flow through distributor systems the manufacturer can't see; promotions planned in one tool and evaluated months later in another"

### Companies/segments to target
1. **Mid-size food & beverage manufacturers (50-200 employees, $10M-$50M)** — demand forecasting pain, inventory management, production scheduling, quality inspection, packaging, regulatory compliance, distributor ordering
2. **Personal care/cosmetics manufacturers (20-100 employees, $5M-$20M)** — SKU proliferation, batch tracking, quality control, regulatory compliance (ingredient labeling), packaging, order processing
3. **Small appliance/hardware manufacturers (30-150 employees, $5M-$30M)** — order processing, inventory, quality control, supplier coordination, shipping/logistics, customer service for orders
4. **Wholesalers/distributors (10-100 employees, $5M-$30M)** — order processing from multiple channels, inventory across locations, price updates, shipping coordination, returns processing, customer quoting

### Pitch
"15.2% of your revenue is being lost to delays, downtime, rework, and quality issues. That's expected to hit 29% by 2030. Only 13% of manufacturers have AI embedded end-to-end — but 37% will by 2030. The ones who move now capture the gap. We deploy AI employees that handle order processing, inventory tracking, quality documentation, compliance reporting, and supplier coordination. First agent in 48 hours. $5K/month. You don't touch tokens or infrastructure."

### Close likelihood: HIGH (60%+)
- Massive documented pain: 15.2% revenue loss today, heading to 29% by 2030 — this is real money
- Proven case studies: PepsiCo ($245M EBITDA from inventory optimization), beverage manufacturer ($46.9M from predictive maintenance), Auxis CPG client ($5-7M from force-ship fix)
- Budget available: $5M-$50M manufacturers can afford $5K/month especially when ROI is proven
- Urgency: "The expectation gap is the strongest signal of urgency we've seen in years" (Schneider Electric)
- Decision-maker accessible: owners, COOs, operations directors — reachable via industry associations, LinkedIn, trade shows
- Nick's computer-use angle: manufacturers often have legacy systems with no API — exactly where computer-use agents shine. "If you can describe a process in words... you can create a computer use agent for it with natural language"
- Entering wedge: order processing, inventory tracking, compliance documentation — high-volume, rules-based, low-risk

### How Twinthos/Dewey helps — 6 distinct ways
1. **Order entry agent** — Receives POs from email, fax, portal, sales reps. Enters into ERP/system. Confirms with customer. Flags anomalies. Operates 24/7. Addresses: "PO processing from multiple channels" bottleneck.
2. **Inventory reorder agent** — Monitors stock levels across locations. Triggers purchase orders when thresholds hit. Coordinates with suppliers. Tracks lead times. Adjusts for demand signals. Prevents stockouts and excess inventory. Addresses: inventory management, supplier coordination.
3. **Quality documentation agent** — Compiles inspection reports from various sources. Generates quality certificates. Tracks lot/batch information. Files compliance documentation. Flags quality issues. Addresses: "100% manual QC" bottleneck, quality documentation.
4. **Shipping/logistics agent** — Tracks shipments across carriers. Updates customers on status. Flags delays. Coordinates with warehouse. Generates shipping documentation. Addresses: shipping coordination bottleneck, customer service for order status.
5. **Compliance reporting agent** — Generates required regulatory reports (FDA, OSHA, DOT, etc.). Tracks compliance deadlines. Maintains documentation. Files certificates. Addresses: compliance documentation bottleneck, regulatory burden.
6. **Supplier coordination agent** — Monitors supplier lead times. Follows up on pending orders. Tracks deliveries. Flags quality issues from supplier shipments. Communicates with suppliers. Addresses: supplier communication, procurement coordination.

